---
import ContentPage from '../components/ContentPage.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { articleSchemas, howToSchema, type FaqItem } from '../seo';
import { SITE_PAGES } from '../site-content';

const page = SITE_PAGES.install;
const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: page.label, path: page.path }];
const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: 'Does NorthCinder need a NorthCinder account?',
    answer: 'No. The initializer creates buyer-owned local configuration. The repository owner does not operate an account system or hosted control plane.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does NorthCinder need my AI-provider API key?',
    answer: 'No. Your AI-provider credentials stay in the AI app that launches the MCP server. NorthCinder does not ask for or proxy that key.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Will every store work immediately after installation?',
    answer: 'No. Store access depends on the adapters you configure and each platform\'s current requirements. NorthCinder reports unavailable and unconfigured stores in its coverage response.',
  },
];
const howTo = howToSchema(page);
const schemas = articleSchemas(page, breadcrumbs, faqs, howTo ? [howTo] : []);
---

<ContentPage
  page={page}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schemas={schemas}
  intro="Install NorthCinder with one public npx command, then connect the generated local MCP configuration to an AI app you already use. The initializer creates buyer-owned configuration and prints the exact command your app should launch. It does not create a NorthCinder account, send your AI-provider key anywhere, or silently claim store access you have not configured."
>
  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <p>
    NorthCinder requires Node.js 20 or later and an AI application that can launch a local Model Context
    Protocol server over standard input and output. The public launcher contains the built client, service,
    shared packages, and store adapters needed for local operation. You do not need to clone the repository
    for the ordinary setup path.
  </p>

  <div class="ledger-list">
    <dl class="ledger-row">
      <dt>Runtime</dt>
      <dd>Node.js 20 or later</dd>
    </dl>
    <dl class="ledger-row">
      <dt>Host</dt>
      <dd>An AI app with local MCP server support</dd>
    </dl>
    <dl class="ledger-row">
      <dt>NorthCinder account</dt>
      <dd>Not required and not offered</dd>
    </dl>
    <dl class="ledger-row">
      <dt>AI-provider key</dt>
      <dd>Remains in your AI app</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Run the initializer</h2>
  <p>Open a terminal under the operating-system account that will run your AI app, then run:</p>

  <pre><code>npx northcinder init</code></pre>

  <p>
    The initializer asks for the local operating mode and store connections you want to prepare. It writes
    owner-only configuration under your chosen config directory. Ordinary local mode is keyless. The initializer
    prints one MCP-host entry. That entry starts the client and owned engine in one process on ephemeral loopback.
    There is no fixed port or second service command.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Add the generated MCP entry</h2>
  <p>
    Copy the generated entry into the MCP configuration used by your AI app. Keep the command and environment
    values together. Local setup needs no key. A separately self-hosted engine uses buyer-generated keys. Restart
    or reload the AI app according to its own MCP instructions, then confirm that the NorthCinder tools appear.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      A healthy connection proves that your AI app can launch the local MCP process. It does not prove that
      every external store credential or platform approval is available.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Start with a constrained brief</h2>
  <p>
    A useful first request states the product, hard requirements, budget, and comparison factors. Specific
    briefs produce evidence you can inspect and make rejected offers easier to understand.
  </p>

  <pre><code>Find black wool running shoes under $130.
Compare price, delivery, fit, and merchant trust.
Explain why the winner ranked first and list the options ruled out.</code></pre>

  <p>
    The response should include ranked offers, reasons, rejected requirements, merchant evidence, source
    labels, and a store coverage report. If a configured adapter is blocked or unavailable, that state should
    appear in the response instead of being hidden behind a partial result.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Configure store access deliberately</h2>
  <p>
    WooCommerce can work with stores that expose the public Store API. Shopify, eBay, and Etsy have additional
    platform-controlled requirements. Amazon comparison uses a buyer-controlled browser profile and remains
    read only; it stops when a challenge appears. Read the <a href="/store-coverage/">store coverage page</a>
    before treating an adapter implementation as live access.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting the first connection</h2>
  <p>
    If the tools do not appear, first check the Node.js version and the command copied into the AI app. Run
    <code>npx northcinder --version</code> in the same environment to confirm the public launcher resolves.
    Then inspect the AI app's local MCP logs for a sanitized startup error. Never post config files without
    removing approval URLs, store credentials, browser-profile paths, and audit data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Re-run the initializer when you intentionally want a new configuration. Preserve an existing config
    directory if it contains profiles, audit history, orders, watches, or a nonce ledger you still need.
    NorthCinder's local state is part of the buyer's record, not a disposable cloud cache.
  </p>

  <FaqList items={faqs} />
  <RelatedLinks links={[
    { href: '/store-coverage/', label: 'Check store coverage', detail: 'See the current adapter and platform boundaries.' },
    { href: '/ranking/', label: 'Understand the ranking', detail: 'Inspect filters, weights, reasons, and sponsorship handling.' },
    { href: '/checkout-safety/', label: 'Review purchase approval', detail: 'See what must happen before a checkout rail can run.' },
  ]} />
</ContentPage>
